%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%
\section{Introduction}
\label{sec:introduction}
%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%

A writer's maxim says,
``Clear writing is evidence of clear thinking.''
This style guide provides helpful hints and suggestions
for developing a clear writing style
the showcases your clear thinking
when writing academic papers and
journal articles with Prof.\ Heun.


%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%
\section{Structure}
\label{sec:structure}
%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%

The five sections of a journal article are almost always

%
\begin{enumerate}

  \item Introduction,

  \item Methods and Data,

  \item Results,

  \item Discussion, and

  \item Conclusion.

\end{enumerate}
%

The broad picture should be explained first
before mentioning any details.

%++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++
\subsection{Introduction}
%++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++

%
\begin{itemize}

  \item The Introduction should start with identifying why and how
        the topic of the paper is important.

  \item The Introduction should then narrow
        to the research question to be answered
        by the paper.

  \item The Introduction should include a short summary of prior literature.

  \item The Introduction should identify a few (typically 3 or less)
        contributions to the literature.

  \item The introduction almost always ends with a paragraph such as the following.
        \begin{quotation}
          \noindent{}The remainder of this paper is structured as follows.
          Section~2 describes methods and data.
          Section~3 describes results obtained
          by applying the methods of Section~2 to the data.
          Section~4 discusses the results, and
          Section~5 concludes.
          Appendices,
          supplementary information, and
          a data repository contain additional information.
        \end{quotation}

\end{itemize}
%


%++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++
\subsection{Results}
\label{ssc:Results}
%++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++

%
\begin{itemize}

  \item The Results section should have at least one result
        for each contribution to the literature.
  \item Write the results section before the rest
        of the paper. Avoid the temptation
        to write methods
        within the results section.
        For example,
        ``Table~3 presents results for three efficiency types.''
        belongs in the results section, not the methods section.
        The results section should discuss the numerical results
        but the methods section should discuss the three methods
        used to determine the numerical results.

\end{itemize}
%


%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%
\section{Style and usage}
\label{sec:style_usage}
%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%

%++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++
\subsection{This, that, or it as the subject of a sentence}
%++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++

Using ``this,'' ``that,'' or ``it'' as the subject of a sentence
is almost always problematic, because
the antecedent is almost always \emph{un}clear to the reader.
(Interestingly, the antecedent is almost always clear to the writer,
making this problem difficult to spot.)
%
\begin{itemize}

  \item Never use this, that, or it as the subject of a sentence.\\
        \emph{\st{This is almost always problematic.}}

  \item This or that as a modifier to the subject is usually OK. \\
        \emph{This writing style is almost always problematic.}

\end{itemize}
%
Note that use of this, that, or it as the subject of a sentence
is MKH's pet peeve.
He apologizes in advance for any roastings that occur.


%++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++
\subsection{Oxford comma}
\label{sec:oxford_comma}
%++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++

The Oxford comma usually clarifies writing.
%
\begin{itemize}

  \item Omission of the Oxford comma leads to confusion. \\
        \emph{\st{I dedicate this book to my parents, Ayn Rand and God.}}

  \item Use the Oxford comma. \\
        \emph{I dedicate this book to my parents, Ayn Rand, and God.}

\end{itemize}


%++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++
\subsection{In order to \ldots}
\label{sec:in_order_to}
%++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++

\begin{itemize}

  \item ``In order to'' is unnecessarily long. \\
        \emph{\st{In order to graduate, earn high marks in all your courses.}}

  \item Simplify by deleting ``In order.''  \\
        \emph{To graduate, earn high marks in all your courses.}

\end{itemize}



%++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++
\subsection{Punctuation tips}
\label{sec:punctuation_tips}
%++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++

\begin{itemize}

  \item LaTeX assumes any period is the end of a sentence and
        will add a little extra space.\\
        \emph{\st{The U.S. was founded in 1776.}} \\
        Add a backslash after periods that don't end a sentence.\\
        \emph{The U.S.\ was founded in 1776.}

\end{itemize}
%++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++
\subsection{Punctuation around quotation marks}
\label{sec:punctuation_quotes}
%++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++

%
\begin{itemize}

  \item \emph{\st{If you ``live another day'', you don't die another day.}}

  \item Punctuation goes inside outside quotation marks. \\
        \emph{If you ``live another day,''
        you don't \href{https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Die_Another_Day}{Die another day}.}

\end{itemize}
%


%++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++
\subsection{Primary, final, and useful}
\label{sec:pfu}
%++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++

``Primary,'' ``final,'' and ``useful''
are adjectives that describe stages in the energy conversion chain
and
should be treated as reserved words.
%
\begin{itemize}

  \item Reserve ``primary,'' ``final,'' and ``useful''
        for the energy conversion chain. \\
        \emph{\st{Equation 12 is useful.}}

  \item Use synonyms such as ``helpful.'' \\
        \emph{Equation 12 is helpful.}

\end{itemize}
%

%++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++
\subsection{Common Words and Phrases to Avoid}
\label{sec:words_to_avoid}
%++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++

When writing a research paper,
certain words may come across
as passive.
Certainty and clarity help the reader comprehend information
more easily.

\begin{itemize}

  \item When referring to figures, tables, etc.\ avoid words
        such as seen, display, etc.\\
        \emph{\st{In this section we display results}}\\
        \emph{In this section we present results} \\
        \emph{\st{It can be seen in Figure~3, \ldots}}\\
        \emph{Figure~3 shows \ldots}

  \item Take care in the placement of adverbs. \\
        \emph{\st{Figure X only shows three concepts.}} \\
        \emph{Figure X shows only three concepts.}

\end{itemize}


%++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++
\subsection{Typesetting variables and matrices}
\label{sec:typesetting_vars_mats}
%++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++

\begin{itemize}

  \item Variables are typeset as italics ($r$).

  \item Vectors are typeset as bold lowercase letters (\textbf{r}).

  \item Matrices are typeset as bold uppercase letters (\textbf{R}).

\end{itemize}


%++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++
\subsection{Punctuation around equations}
\label{sec:punctuation_equations}
%++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++

Punctuate equations as though they are part of the sentence
and the equal sign is a verb.
%
\begin{itemize}

  \item Newton was the first to understand that
        \begin{equation}
          \cancel{F = ma}
        \end{equation}
        \noindent{} although others had considered the idea.

  \item Newton was the first to understand that
        \begin{equation}
          F = ma \, ,
        \end{equation}
        \noindent{} although others had considered the idea.

  \item Add a little space between the equation and the punctuation with \verb+\,+~.

\end{itemize}
%


%++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++
\subsection{Narrating equations}
\label{sec:narrating_equations}
%++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++

In the \texttt{Methods} section,
first state the equation, then explain terms.

%
\begin{itemize}

  \item Not like this:\\
        \st{The exergy of a mixture ($B_{mix}$)
        is the ambient
        temperature ($T_{0}$) multiplied by the change
        in entropy of the mixture from the Earth's
        crust to the current state ($S_{mix}$).}

\begin{equation}
  \cancel{B_{mix} =
                    T_{0}S_{mix} =
                    -RT_{0}\sum_{i}{N_{i}{y_{i}}\ln{y_{i}}}}
\end{equation}

  \item Rather like this:\\
        The exergy of a mixture ($B_{mix}$) is given by
\begin{equation}
  {B_{mix} =
              T_{0}S_{mix} =
              -RT_{0}\sum_{i}{N_{i}{y_{i}}\ln{y_{i}}}} \, ,
\end{equation}
%
where
$T_0$ is the ambient temperature,
$S_{mix}$ is total mixture entropy,
$R$ is the ideal gas constant,
$N_i$ is the number of moles of mixture component $i$, and
$y_i$ is the mole fraction of mixture component $i$.

\end{itemize}


%++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++
\subsection{Units}
\label{sec:units}
%++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++

\begin{itemize}

  \item All numbers that deserve units should have units!

  \item Units should not be surrounded by \verb+()+,
        \verb+[]+, or \verb+{}+. \\
        \st{42~[m]} \\
        42~m

  \item Units should not be italicized so should not be typeset
        in \LaTeX's math mode.

  \item In \LaTeX, dollar signs (\$) are unnecessary to format units,
        because units should not be italicized.
        Also, use ``/'' not \texttt{\textbackslash{}frac\{\}\{\}}. \\
        \st{\texttt{\textdollar{}kJ/kg\textdollar}} \\
        \st{\texttt{\textdollar{}\textbackslash{}frac\{kJ\}\{kg\}\textdollar}} \\
        Instead, simply \texttt{kJ/kg}.

  \item When two or more units are multiplied in a numerator or denominator
        use the interpunct instead of a hyphen or multiplication sign.
        Like this:\\
        $\mathrm{kJ/mol \! \cdot \! K}$
        %
        where the interpunct comes from \texttt{\textbackslash cdot}.

  \item You may need to use \verb+\!+ to close up
        leading and trailing space around
        the \texttt{\textbackslash cdot},
        as in the example above.

  \item To include subscripts on units, use the code:
        \verb+$\mathrm{kJ_i/mol_i}$+ which renders as
        $\mathrm{kJ_i/mol_i}$.

\end{itemize}


%++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++
\subsection{Other aspects of style}
\label{sec:other_aspects_style}
%++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++

\begin{itemize}

  \item Move most numerical results to a table or graph.
        Tables and graphs are the best way
        to highlight important results,
        because they draw the reader's eye.

  \item Use subsections with headings to provide
        guidance to the reader.

\end{itemize}


%++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++
\subsection{Other aspects of formatting}
\label{sec:other_aspects_formatting}
%++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++

If you don't (or can't) use \LaTeX{}
(which does these things automatically),
follow these rules:

\begin{itemize}

  \item Center equations and put equation numbers at right margin.
        See textbooks for formatting.
        Do not include the word ``equation''
        with the label.

  \item Center page numbers at the bottom,
        starting with 1 on the first page of content.
        (The cover page does not deserve a number!)
        Don't number the empty page between cover page and first page of content.
        See a real book for how to number pages.

  \item Fully justify text (left and right sides)
        to achieve a professional look.
        Look at real books to see how they are typeset.

  \item Center table captions above the table.

  \item Center figure captions beneath the figure.

  \item Don't orphan tables across pages.

\end{itemize}


%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%
\section{The \LaTeX{} technology stack}
\label{sec:use_latex}
%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%

Writing in \LaTeX{} makes your life easier.
\LaTeX{} is much better than word
for equations and overall document formatting.
You can also use \texttt{R} and RStudio
with \texttt{knitr} or \texttt{Sweave} to embed
\texttt{R} code within \LaTeX documents.

\begin{itemize}

  \item Install \LaTeX from \url{https://tug.org/texlive/}.
        (Don't install \LaTeX from MikTeX.)

  \item Install R from \url{https://www.r-project.org}.

  \item Use RStudio or Positron as your IDE.
        RStudio is older and more mature.
        Positron is the future IDE for \texttt{R} and Python data science.
        Install RStudio from \url{https://posit.co/downloads/}.
        Install Positron from \url{https://positron.posit.co/download.html}.

  \item In \LaTeX source code, use short lines.

  \item Start each sentence on a new line.

  \item Break lines after punctuation and conjunctions.

  \item Use \texttt{BibLaTeX} for bibliography management and citations.
    \begin{itemize}
      \item On the Mac, use the BibDesk app to edit and organize references.

      \item The native file format for BibDesk is a .bib file
            compatible with BibLaTeX.
            Standard naming
            for this file is \texttt{references.bib}.

      \item For citations with two authors, use the format \\
            \texttt{author = \{A. \{Valero Capilla\}
                    and A. \{Valero Delgado\}\}} \\
            in the \texttt{.bib} file.

      \item Use \texttt{\{\}} around compound names,
            as in the example above.

      \item The \texttt{.bib} file will not compile
            if the citekey contains \texttt{\&} or \texttt{;} .

      \item Use the following commands to cite in the paper: \\
            \texttt{\textbackslash citep\{citekey\}} or
            \texttt{\textbackslash citet\{citekey\}}.
            ``t'' is gives textual citation and includes
            the author before the year.
            ``p'' puts both author and year in parentheses.

      \item To cite a table or figure from a paper,
            use the command \\
            \texttt{\textbackslash citet[Table$\sim$X.X]\{citekey\}} or
            \texttt{\textbackslash citep[Figure$\sim$X.X]\{citekey\}}.

      \item To cite a table, figure, or page from a paper,
            use ``\verb+~+'' to include non-breaking
            and appropriate-length space between the period and the number:
            [p.~16] not \st{[p.16]} or \st{[p. 16]}.

    \end{itemize}

    \item For single-character subscripts,
          do not surround with braces \st{\texttt{\textdollar{}P\textunderscore\{0\}\textdollar}}.
          Instead, \verb+$P_0$+ is sufficient and less verbose.

    \item Don't label an equation
          unless you refer to it in the text.

    \item Equation and section labels should never have
          any spaces.

    \item Label all equations with the prefix \texttt{eq:}.\\
          Label all figures with the prefix \texttt{fig:}.\\
          Label all tables with the prefix \texttt{tab:}.\\

    \item Eliminate all non-ASCII characters from
          \texttt{.tex} and \texttt{.Rnw} files.
          Use the \texttt{R} command \\
          \verb+tools::showNonASCIIfile(file = "file_name")+ \\
          to find non-ASCII characters.

\end{itemize}


%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%
\section{Helpful hints}
\label{sec:helpful_hints}
%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%

%++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++
\subsection{Type expansion}
\label{sec:type_expansion}
%++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++

Use a snippet manager or type expansion app to help with complicated
\LaTeX{} expressions.
For example, the snippet manager can be configured so that
typing \texttt{llga} produces the following.

\begin{lstlisting}[language=TEX, caption=\texttt{llga} example.]
\alpha
\end{lstlisting}

A more complicated example
involves configuring the snippet manager so that
typing \texttt{llsec} generates the following.

\begin{lstlisting}[language=TEX, caption=\texttt{llsec} example.]
%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%
\section{}
\label{sec:placeholder_label}
%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%
\end{lstlisting}

Use the prefix ``ll'' for \LaTeX{} commands and
the prefix ``llg'' for Greek letters.

RStudio includes a snippet manager found in "Edit Code Snippets".
Pick which file type you want to incorporate the type expansion in.
For more information, visit
\href{https://support.posit.co/hc/en-us/articles/204463668-Code-Snippets-in-the-RStudio-IDE}{RStudio snippets}.

macOS includes type expansion in the Keyboard area of settings.
Tools such as \href{https://www.alfredapp.com}{Alfred} and
\href{https://textexpander.com}{Text expander}
provide a way to specify the desired
cursor location after snippet expansion.


%++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++
\subsection{Installing Packages}
\label{sec:installing_packages}
%++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++

%------------------------------
\subsubsection{\LaTeX{} packages}
\label{sec:latex_packages}
%------------------------------

\url{https://ctan.org} is a website for LaTeX packages.
In the preamble of the \LaTeX{} file,
include \verb+\usepackage{package_name}+.

For chemical equations,
packages \texttt{mchem} or \verb+flutter_tex+
are useful.
\href{https://ctan.math.illinois.edu/macros/latex/contrib/mhchem/mhchem.pdf}
provides the manual for mhchem.


%------------------------------
\subsubsection{\texttt{R} packages}
\label{sec:r_packages}
%------------------------------

Under the packages tab
in the terminal of RStudio,
there is an install tab
and a list of available \texttt{R} packages.


